<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="fatty acid β-oxidation IV (unsaturated, even number)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5138" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Some of the most prevalent fatty acids in lipid storage tissues, such as |FRAME: LINOLEIC_ACID| and |FRAME: LINOLENIC_ACID|, contain &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;-double bonds on even-numbered carbons. When such fatty acids are processed by the first enzyme of the core &amp;beta;-oxidation pathway, |FRAME: CPLX-7064| (see |FRAME: PWY-5136|), they are converted into |FRAME: 2-trans-4-cis-dienoyl-CoAs|. This intermediate cannot proceed directly through the core &amp;beta;-oxidation pathway, and must be processed via either of two alternative routes. In the first route |FRAME: 2-trans-4-cis-dienoyl-CoAs| enters the so called reductase/isomerase pathway, which involves the enzymes |FRAME: MONOMER-12061| and |FRAME: CPLX-7065|. In this route the dienoyl-CoA is converted to |FRAME: TRANS-D2-ENOYL-COA|, which can be processed further by the core &amp;beta;-oxidation pathway. Alternatively, |FRAME: 2-trans-4-cis-dienoyl-CoAs| can be converted to |FRAME: Cis-2-enoyl-CoAs| through one more cycle of core beta-oxidation pathway. A &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;-2-enoyl-CoA cannot be completely degraded via the core &amp;beta;-oxidation pathway since its hydratation yields |FRAME: D-3-HYDROXYACYL-COA| which is not a substrate for |FRAME:ENZRXN-12592|. Thus, the D-3-hydroxyacyl-CoA enters the so called epimerase pathway that involves converting |FRAME: D-3-HYDROXYACYL-COA| to |FRAME: L-3-HYDROXYACYL-COA| by |FRAME: ENZRXN-12606|. Alternatively, this conversion can be performed by two stereo-specific enoyl-CoA hydratases. The L-3-hydroxyacyl-CoA then returns to the core &amp;beta;-oxidation pathway for complete degradation. The |FRAME:EC-5.3.3.8 &quot;enoyl-CoA isomerase&quot;| and |FRAME: EC-5.1.2.3 &quot;3-hydroxyacyl-CoA epimerase&quot;| activities, together with two enzyme activities also found in the core &amp;beta;-oxidation pathway, namely |FRAME: EC-4.2.1.17 &quot;2-enoyl-CoA hydratase&quot;| and |FRAME:EC-1.1.1.35 &quot;3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase&quot;|, are attributed to a single multifunctional protein (MFP). Studies in |FRAME: TAX-3659| cotyledon suggested the epimerase pathway is much more effective than the reductase/isomerase pathway |CITS: [1879422]|, whereas studies in |FRAME: ORG-5993| indicated that both pathways have a similar contribution |CITS: [11080293]|.</Description>
